# Batch 28 (2026-11-15): things that fall from the sky and ships without crews (Fortean puzzles, feral children, a sideshow mermaid);
# UFO cases and sky events that changed minds (1833 Leonids, Shoemaker-Levy 9, Kepler's star, cold fusion, the spaghetti tree);
# Africa and the Americas (Bantu expansion, Benin walls, Tula, El Mirador, Paquime, Manco Capac) with Asian sky and bird myths;
# Mesopotamia, Egypt and prehistoric/medieval Britain (Uruk, Merneptah Stele, Dendera zodiac, Amesbury Archer, Seahenge, hill giants, Arthur's grave)
H,M='history','myth'
def X(id,title,lane,status,pr,start,ev,main,alts,src,img=None,end=None,changed=None,dateNote=None):
  d=dict(id=id,title=title,lane=lane,status=status,priority=pr,start=start,end=end,ev=ev,main=main,alts=alts,src=src,img=img)
  if changed:d['changed']=changed
  if dateNote:d['dateNote']=dateNote
  return d
E=[]
# --- part 1: falls from the sky, ghost ships, feral children, unsolved and faked
E+=[
X('rain-of-animals','Raining fish and frogs: animal rains from the Bible to Honduras',H,'disputed',2,1625,['eyewitness accounts','textual','meteorological (hypothesis)'],
 "Reports of small animals, usually fish or frogs, falling with rain go back to antiquity. The Bible describes a plague of frogs in Egypt and quail falling to feed the Israelites; the Greek writer Athenaeus mentions a three-day rain of fish near Chaeronea. A rain of frogs was reported at Tournai (Belgium) in 1625, French soldiers described toads falling during heavy rain near Lille in 1794, and in October 1987 residents of three Gloucestershire towns reported tiny pink frogs falling with rain. In Yoro, Honduras, people speak of a yearly 'lluvia de peces' (rain of fish) and hold a festival for it. Animal rains became a favourite subject of the American collector of anomalies Charles Fort in the early 20th century.",
 [('Waterspouts and whirlwinds','The most common scientific suggestion, made as early as André-Marie Ampère in the 19th century, is that waterspouts or strong winds lift animals from ponds or the sea and drop them miles away. This fits the fact that falls usually involve a single species of small animal, but the lifting itself has never been directly observed by scientists.'),
  ('No fall at all','Another explanation is that many animals were already on the ground (for example frogs emerging after rain, or fish stranded when floodwaters retreat) and were assumed to have fallen. Locals in Yoro say the fish appear after storms near streams.'),
  ('Fortean view','Charles Fort and later writers collected such cases as evidence that science ignores data that do not fit. Fort half-jokingly proposed a "Super-Sargasso Sea" in the sky. These ideas are not taken seriously by scientists, but the collections preserved many old reports.')],
 ['wiki:Rain_of_animals','wiki:Charles_Fort'],img='File:Rain of frogs.jpg',dateNote='Reports from antiquity to today; 1625 Tournai shown'),
X('kentucky-meat-shower','The Kentucky meat shower of 1876',H,'confirmed',2,1876,['newspaper reports','laboratory analysis'],
 "Between 11 a.m. and noon on 3 March 1876, flakes of what looked like fresh red meat fell from a clear sky over an area roughly 90 by 45 metres near Olympia Springs in Bath County, Kentucky. Mrs Crouch, who was making soap outside her house, saw it fall. Newspapers reported that two men tasted it and thought it was mutton or venison. Samples were sent to scientists: different analysts identified lung tissue, muscle and cartilage, and suggestions about its source included beef, lamb, deer, bear, horse and even human infant tissue. The exact type of meat was never settled.",
 [('Vomiting vultures','The most widely cited explanation, put forward by the Kentucky physician L. D. Kastenbine at the time, is that a flock of vultures threw up their meals in flight, as vultures sometimes do when startled or trying to take off quickly.'),
  ('Nostoc','One early analyst suggested the material was Nostoc, a cyanobacterium that swells into jelly-like masses after rain. Later tests that found animal tissue made this unlikely.'),
  ('Unexplained','For collectors of anomalies the case remains a classic "fall from the sky" because nobody saw birds overhead. Sceptics reply that a few high-flying birds could easily have been missed.')],
 ['wiki:Kentucky_meat_shower'],img=None),
X('spontaneous-human-combustion','Spontaneous human combustion: bodies found burned in unburned rooms',H,'debunked',2,1951,['forensic','medical reports','literary'],
 "Since the 17th century there have been reports of people found almost entirely burned to ash while the room around them, and sometimes their lower legs, were left largely untouched. The idea that a human body could burst into flame without an outside source became famous through fiction: Charles Dickens killed off the character Krook this way in Bleak House (1852-53) and defended the idea in his preface. A much-discussed modern case was Mary Reeser, found burned in St Petersburg, Florida in 1951. Investigators note common features in such cases: the victims were usually elderly, alone, often drinking or smoking, and near a heat source such as a fireplace, candle or cigarette.",
 [('The wick effect (mainstream)','Experiments, including tests with pig carcasses wrapped in cloth, show that a body can burn slowly for hours like an inside-out candle: clothing acts as a wick and melted body fat as fuel. The fire stays concentrated on the body, explaining why the surroundings may survive. Scientists conclude that cases involve an overlooked external ignition source.'),
  ('Something unknown','Believers have proposed unknown internal causes, such as ball lightning, static electricity, intestinal gases or exotic particles. None has been demonstrated.'),
  ('A pattern of the era','Historians point out that 18th- and 19th-century temperance writers used the idea as a warning: heavy drinkers, they claimed, could ignite. This moral framing shaped which cases were reported.')],
 ['wiki:Spontaneous_human_combustion','wiki:Wick_effect'],img='Spontaneous human combustion',dateNote='Reports from the 17th century on; 1951 Reeser case shown'),
X('devils-sea','The Devil\'s Sea or Dragon\'s Triangle: the Pacific\'s "Bermuda Triangle"',H,'debunked',2,1952,['newspaper reports','official investigation'],
 "Ma no Umi, 'devil sea' or 'dangerous sea', is a general Japanese term for treacherous waters. In January 1955 Japanese newspapers used it for the sea south of Tokyo around the Izu and Ogasawara islands after a ship, the Shinyo Maru No. 10, lost radio contact (it was found safe days later); the Yomiuri Shimbun mapped nine ships lost in five years. The New York Times reported it as 'The Devil's Sea' where ships had vanished 'in perfect weather'. The research ship Kaiyo Maru No. 5, sent in 1952 to study the erupting undersea volcano Myojin-sho, was lost with all aboard. In 1974 Charles Berlitz added the area to his book The Bermuda Triangle, and in 1989 he wrote The Dragon's Triangle about it.",
 [('Sceptical investigation','The librarian Larry Kusche contacted Japanese authorities in the 1970s and found no one who knew of a special danger zone beyond a small area around Myojin-sho. The Kaiyo Maru No. 5 was destroyed by the volcano\'s eruption; most of the other lost vessels were small fishing boats with poor radios, the weather was not perfect, and some lay far outside the area.'),
  ('Paranormal zone','Berlitz and others linked the area with the Bermuda Triangle as one of several "vile vortices" on Earth, suggesting magnetic anomalies or unknown forces. There is no evidence that ships are lost there at a higher rate than in other busy, stormy, volcanically active seas.')],
 ['wiki:Devil\'s_Sea','wiki:Bermuda_Triangle'],img='Devil\'s Sea'),
X('carroll-deering','The Carroll A. Deering: a schooner found with sails set and no crew',H,'confirmed',2,1921,['official investigation','newspaper reports'],
 "On 31 January 1921 the five-masted schooner Carroll A. Deering was found hard aground with all sails set on Diamond Shoals off Cape Hatteras, North Carolina. When she was finally boarded on 4 February, her crew of eleven, their belongings, the log, navigation equipment and both lifeboats were gone; food appeared to have been prepared for the next day's meal and the steering gear was damaged. Days earlier, a lightship keeper at Cape Lookout had been hailed by a red-haired man with a foreign accent who said the ship had lost her anchors. Five US government departments investigated. A message in a bottle claiming the ship had been captured turned out to be a forgery by a local fisherman. The inquiry closed in 1922 without an official finding; the wreck was dynamited as a hazard.",
 [('Mutiny','The first mate had been heard threatening the elderly captain in Barbados, and mutiny became the explanation favoured by investigators, though it was never proven.'),
  ('Pirates or rum-runners','Prohibition-era smugglers, or pirates (some suggested Bolshevik sympathisers), were blamed in the press. A mysterious steamer seen near the ship that ignored signals fuelled this idea.'),
  ('Bermuda Triangle','Later paranormal writers added the Deering to Bermuda Triangle lists, although Cape Hatteras lies well outside the usual triangle and the ship was lost on notoriously dangerous shoals.')],
 ['wiki:Carroll_A._Deering'],img='Carroll A. Deering'),
X('baychimo','SS Baychimo: the ghost ship that drifted in Arctic ice for 38 years',H,'confirmed',2,1931,['eyewitness reports','official documents'],
 "The Baychimo was a steel cargo steamer built in Sweden in 1914 and owned by the Hudson's Bay Company, which used her to trade supplies for furs with Inuit settlements in the Canadian Arctic. In October 1931 she became trapped in pack ice off Alaska near Barrow. Most of the crew were flown out, while some stayed in a shelter nearby to wait for spring. After a blizzard in November the ship vanished; the company assumed she had sunk and abandoned her. But the Baychimo was sighted drifting along the Alaskan coast many times afterwards, boarded by Inuit hunters and others more than once, and last reported in 1969, still frozen in ice. Searches have never found her.",
 [('Why she survived','Sturdy construction and the slow drift of sea ice explain how an unmanned steel ship could stay afloat for decades; ships frozen in ice can travel great distances without sinking.'),
  ('A true ghost-ship story','Unlike legendary ghost ships such as the Flying Dutchman, the Baychimo is documented. She is often listed alongside mysteries such as the Mary Celeste, but there is no mystery about why she was abandoned, only about where she ended up.')],
 ['wiki:SS_Baychimo'],img='SS Baychimo',end=1969),
X('victor-of-aveyron','Victor of Aveyron: the wild boy of the woods',H,'confirmed',2,1800,['medical reports','textual'],
 "In January 1800 a naked boy, thought to be roughly nine to twelve years old, who had apparently lived alone in the woods of southern France for years, came out of the forest near Saint-Sernin-sur-Rance in the Aveyron. He did not speak, and he had escaped from people several times before. In Paris the young doctor Jean Marc Gaspard Itard took charge of him, named him Victor and spent five years trying to teach him language and social behaviour, publishing detailed reports. Victor learned to recognise some words and showed affection, but never learned to speak more than a few sounds. He died in Paris in 1828. Itard's methods influenced Maria Montessori and the education of children with learning disabilities, and the story inspired François Truffaut's film L'Enfant sauvage (1970).",
 [('A test of human nature','Enlightenment thinkers hoped such a child would show what humans are like without society. Itard concluded that much of what makes us human is learned, and that there may be a critical period for learning language.'),
  ('Disability, not just isolation','Many modern scholars suggest Victor may have been autistic or had another developmental condition, and may have been abandoned because of it, so his difficulties cannot be blamed on isolation alone. This is debated and cannot be proven.')],
 ['wiki:Victor_of_Aveyron','wiki:Feral_child'],img='Victor of Aveyron',dateNote='Emerged from the woods January 1800; died 1828'),
X('hinterkaifeck','The Hinterkaifeck murders: Germany\'s most puzzling unsolved crime',H,'confirmed',2,1922,['police records','newspaper reports'],
 "On the evening of 31 March 1922 six people were killed with a mattock on the remote Hinterkaifeck farm about 70 km north of Munich: the farmer Andreas Gruber, his wife, their widowed daughter Viktoria Gabriel, her two young children and the maid, who had arrived that day. Afterwards the killer apparently stayed at the farm for several days, eating food, feeding the animals and lighting the fire. Before the murders Gruber had reported footprints in the snow leading from the forest to the farm but not back, strange noises in the attic and a missing house key, and a previous maid had left saying the house was haunted. Despite repeated investigations over decades, and a re-examination by police academy students in 2007, the case remains unsolved.",
 [('Suspects','Suspicion fell over the years on a neighbour, Lorenz Schlittenbauer, who may have fathered Viktoria\'s son; on Viktoria\'s husband Karl Gabriel, officially killed in the First World War but rumoured to have survived; and on several local men. None was charged.'),
  ('The uncanny details','The footprints, the noises in the attic and the killer living with the dead have made the case a favourite of true-crime and ghost-story writers. Investigators see them as signs that the murderer had been hiding on the farm before the attack, not as anything supernatural.')],
 ['wiki:Hinterkaifeck_murders'],img='Hinterkaifeck murders'),
X('fiji-mermaid','The Fiji mermaid: P. T. Barnum\'s monkey-and-fish sideshow wonder',H,'debunked',2,1842,['museum objects','newspaper reports'],
 "In 1842 the showman P. T. Barnum exhibited in New York a small, shrivelled 'mermaid' supposedly caught near the Fiji Islands. It was in fact the upper body of a young monkey sewn to the rear half of a fish, a type of object made in East Asia, probably Japan, where craftsmen produced dried 'mermaids' for shrines and for sale. Barnum leased it from the Boston museum owner Moses Kimball and built excitement with planted newspaper stories and a fake British naturalist. The original probably burned in one of the fires at Barnum's museums, but many similar 'Feejee mermaids' survive, including one at Harvard's Peabody Museum.",
 [('Hoax and showmanship','Historians see it as a landmark in commercial hoaxing: Barnum made money both from believers and from people who paid to see how they were being fooled.'),
  ('A link to Japanese mermaid lore','Similar composite mermaid mummies are kept as relics in some Japanese temples, connected with ningyo legends. A 2022-23 study of one such temple mermaid in Okayama found it was made mostly of cloth, paper and cotton with fish skin and scales, showing the craft tradition behind the Barnum object.')],
 ['wiki:Fiji_mermaid','wiki:P._T._Barnum','wiki:Ningyo'],img='Fiji mermaid'),
]
# --- part 2: UFO cases and sky events that changed minds
E+=[
X('lakenheath-1956','Lakenheath-Bentwaters 1956: radar and visual UFO contacts over English airbases',H,'disputed',2,1956,['radar','eyewitness reports','military records'],
 "On the night of 13-14 August 1956, radar operators at the US-run air bases RAF Bentwaters and RAF Lakenheath in eastern England tracked unidentified targets, some apparently moving at thousands of miles per hour, while ground personnel and the pilot of a C-47 reported fast-moving lights. According to the Lakenheath radar watch supervisor, Forrest Perkins, writing to investigators in 1968, two RAF de Havilland Venom interceptors were scrambled; the first pilot made contact, but the target then manoeuvred behind him and followed him for about ten minutes despite evasive action. The night was clear, with many Perseid meteors. The case was recorded in Project Blue Book and later examined by the Condon Committee, which, though dismissive of UFOs in general, wrote that in this case 'the probability that at least one genuine UFO was involved appears to be fairly high'.",
 [('Radar and astronomy','Sceptics, including later analyses of the Blue Book file, argue that the targets were false returns caused by unusual atmospheric conditions (anomalous propagation), combined with misidentified stars, Mars and meteors, and confusion between several separate events.'),
  ('A genuine unknown','Ufologists such as the atmospheric physicist James E. McDonald regarded it as one of the strongest cases ever recorded because radar and visual sightings by trained military personnel seemed to match.')],
 ['wiki:Lakenheath-Bentwaters_incident','wiki:Condon_Report'],img='File:Lakenheath Military airfield - geograph.org.uk - 53241.jpg'),
X('exeter-1965','The Exeter incident: police officers and a teenager see red lights over a New Hampshire field',H,'disputed',2,1965,['eyewitness reports','police report','official investigation'],
 "At about 2 a.m. on 3 September 1965, 18-year-old Norman Muscarello, hitchhiking home near Kensington, New Hampshire, saw a large object with five bright, flashing red lights hovering over a field. He reported it at the Exeter police station; officer Eugene Bertrand, who had earlier met a frightened woman claiming a similar object had followed her car, returned with him to the field, where both, and soon officer David Hunt, saw the lights rise from behind trees as horses and dogs panicked. An Air Force officer sent from nearby Pease Air Force Base reported to Project Blue Book that he could find no probable cause and that the witnesses, especially the two patrolmen, seemed stable and reliable. The writer John G. Fuller's book Incident at Exeter (1966) made the case nationally famous.",
 [('Air-refuelling tanker','In 2011 the sceptic Joe Nickell and retired USAF pilot James McGaha proposed in Skeptical Inquirer that the witnesses saw a KC-97 tanker from nearby Pease Air Force Base, whose five underbelly lights flashed in the same sequence before air-to-air refuelling.'),
  ('Unexplained craft','Many ufologists consider it one of the best-witnessed close sightings, because the witnesses were police officers who described a silent object at low altitude. The case is still celebrated at Exeter\'s UFO festival.')],
 ['wiki:Exeter_incident'],img='File:Incident at Exeter map.png'),
X('gulf-breeze-1987','Gulf Breeze 1987: the UFO photos and the model in the attic',H,'disputed',2,1987,['photographs','newspaper reports'],
 "In November 1987 Ed Walters, a building contractor in Gulf Breeze, Florida, gave the local Gulf Breeze Sentinel photographs of a glowing, structured UFO and later described repeated encounters and abduction experiences. Other residents came forward with sightings, and the town became a magnet for UFO watchers. Some ufologists, including the physicist Bruce Maccabee, analysed the photos and judged them genuine. In 1990, after the Walters family had moved, the new owners of their house found a small styrofoam model UFO hidden in the attic; a reporter, Craig Myers, used it to recreate the photographs closely.",
 [('Hoax','Sceptics and many journalists regard the model as strong evidence that the photos were faked; a young man also claimed to have helped Walters stage pictures.'),
  ('Planted evidence','Walters denied any hoax and said the model had been planted to discredit him. Some supporters point out that other people in the area reported sightings, which they say cannot all be explained by one model.')],
 ['wiki:Gulf_Breeze_UFO_incident'],img='Gulf Breeze UFO incident',end=1988),
X('leonids-1833','The night the stars fell: the Leonid meteor storm of 1833',H,'confirmed',2,1833,['eyewitness accounts','newspaper reports','astronomical'],
 "On the night of 12-13 November 1833, people across North America east of the Rocky Mountains saw a storm of shooting stars, estimated at tens of thousands or more per hour. Many thought the world was ending. The event was recorded in Lakota winter counts, whose calendars were reset by it, and Native American birthdays were reckoned from it; Frederick Douglass and Harriet Tubman were among those who remembered it, and the Mormon leader Joseph Smith saw it as a literal fulfilment of prophecy. The Yale scientist Denison Olmsted collected reports and showed that the meteors radiated from one point in the constellation Leo, helping prove that meteors come from space rather than the atmosphere. The Leonids are now known to come from debris left by comet Tempel-Tuttle, with great storms roughly every 33 years.",
 [('A sign from heaven','Preachers across the United States took the falling stars as a sign of the Last Days (Revelation 6:13: "the stars of heaven fell unto the earth"). The memory of 1833 fed later millennialist movements, and the "night the stars fell" entered African American and frontier folklore.'),
  ('Science born from a spectacle','Historians of astronomy see 1833 as the birth of meteor science: the realisation that showers repeat and come from a fixed direction led to the link with comets later in the century.')],
 ['wiki:Leonids','wiki:Denison_Olmsted','wiki:Winter_count'],img='Leonids'),
X('shoemaker-levy-9','Comet Shoemaker-Levy 9 hits Jupiter (1994)',H,'confirmed',2,1994,['astronomical observation','spacecraft imagery'],
 "Comet Shoemaker-Levy 9 was discovered in March 1993 by Carolyn and Eugene Shoemaker and David Levy at Palomar Observatory. It had been captured by Jupiter's gravity decades earlier and, after passing too close in July 1992, had been torn into a string of fragments up to about 2 km across. Between 16 and 22 July 1994 more than twenty fragments struck Jupiter's southern hemisphere at about 60 km per second, leaving dark scars larger than Earth that were more visible than the Great Red Spot for months. It was the first collision between two Solar System bodies ever observed directly, watched by the Hubble Space Telescope, the Galileo spacecraft and observatories worldwide.",
 [('A wake-up call','The impacts made the danger of cosmic collisions vivid to the public and to politicians. In the years after, NASA\'s programme to find near-Earth objects was expanded, and the idea of Jupiter as a "shield" that sweeps up comets was widely discussed (some later studies question how protective Jupiter really is).'),
  ('Impacts in human history','The event gave new credibility to researchers who argued that impacts had shaped Earth\'s past, from the dinosaur-killing Chicxulub impact to more speculative proposals of recent impacts behind myths and catastrophes.')],
 ['wiki:Comet_Shoemaker–Levy_9'],img='File:Comet Shoemaker-Levy 9 Impact Site on Jupiter.jpg',end=1994),
X('keplers-supernova','Kepler\'s star: the last supernova seen in our galaxy (1604)',H,'confirmed',2,1604,['astronomical records','textual'],
 "In October 1604 a new star appeared in the constellation Ophiuchus, brighter than any other star in the night sky and visible in daylight for weeks. It was observed in Europe, China and Korea. Johannes Kepler studied it closely and published De Stella Nova (On the New Star) in 1606. It appeared close to a conjunction of Jupiter and Saturn, which Kepler connected with astrological ideas and with speculation about the Star of Bethlehem. Coming only 32 years after Tycho Brahe's new star of 1572, it helped overturn the old belief that the heavens beyond the Moon never change. Today it is classified as a Type Ia supernova, an exploding white dwarf, and it remains the most recent supernova in the Milky Way seen with the naked eye.",
 [('Omens and prophecy','Many writers at the time treated the new star as an omen of great political or religious change, and Kepler himself discussed its astrological meaning alongside its physics.'),
  ('Star of Bethlehem theories','Kepler suggested that the Star of Bethlehem might have been a similar new star appearing during a conjunction of planets. Modern astronomers have proposed conjunctions, comets and novae for the biblical star; others see it as a theological symbol, not an astronomical event.')],
 ['wiki:Kepler\'s_Supernova','wiki:Star_of_Bethlehem'],img='Kepler\'s Supernova'),
X('spaghetti-tree','The spaghetti-tree hoax: the BBC fools Britain (1957)',H,'debunked',2,1957,['film','audience research'],
 "On 1 April 1957 the BBC's respected current affairs programme Panorama broadcast a three-minute report, narrated by the trusted broadcaster Richard Dimbleby, showing a Swiss family harvesting spaghetti from trees after a mild winter and the 'virtual disappearance of the spaghetti weevil'. At the time spaghetti was an unusual food in Britain, and many viewers did not know it was made from flour and water. Hundreds phoned the BBC; some asked how to grow their own spaghetti trees and were told to 'place a sprig of spaghetti in a tin of tomato sauce and hope for the best'. CNN later called it the biggest hoax any reputable news organisation ever pulled.",
 [('Why it worked','Media historians point to the trusted presenter, the documentary style and viewers\' unfamiliarity with the subject: an example of how authority and format can make almost anything believable.'),
  ('Harmless fun or warning','Some saw it as a joke in poor taste for a serious news programme; it is now widely celebrated as one of the greatest April Fools\' pranks and used to teach media literacy.')],
 ['wiki:Spaghetti-tree_hoax','wiki:April_Fools\'_Day'],img='Spaghetti-tree hoax'),
X('cold-fusion-1989','Cold fusion (1989): energy from a jar of heavy water?',H,'debunked',2,1989,['experimental','peer-reviewed'],
 "On 23 March 1989 the electrochemists Martin Fleischmann and Stanley Pons announced at a press conference at the University of Utah that a simple tabletop cell, passing electric current through heavy water with a palladium electrode, produced more heat than could be explained by chemistry, and traces of neutrons and tritium. They suggested nuclear fusion was happening at room temperature, raising hopes of cheap, limitless energy. Laboratories around the world rushed to repeat the experiment. A few early positive results were withdrawn, most attempts failed, and flaws were found in the original measurements. By the end of 1989 most scientists considered the claim dead; reviews by the US Department of Energy in 1989 and 2004 found no convincing evidence.",
 [('Pathological science','Most physicists regard cold fusion as a case study in how announcing results before peer review, combined with wishful thinking and measurement errors, can lead science astray.'),
  ('Ongoing research','A small community continues to investigate "low-energy nuclear reactions" (LENR), reporting anomalous heat. A large Google-funded project published in 2019 found no evidence of cold fusion but improved materials science; supporters say the question is not completely closed.')],
 ['wiki:Cold_fusion','doi:10.1016/0022-0728(89)80006-3'],img='Cold fusion'),
]
# --- part 3: Africa and the Americas; Asian star and bird myths
E+=[
X('bantu-expansion','The Bantu expansion: how one language family spread across half a continent',H,'confirmed',1,-4000,['linguistic','genetic (ancient DNA)','archaeological'],
 "Around 500 languages spoken today across central, eastern and southern Africa, from Swahili and Kikongo to isiZulu and Shona, belong to the Bantu family and are strikingly similar, pointing to a shared origin in the region of today's south-eastern Nigeria and western Cameroon. Over roughly 5,000 years, beginning around 4000 BCE, Bantu-speaking farmers spread south through or around the Congo rainforest and east towards the Great Lakes, bringing farming, pottery and later iron-working. By about 300 CE they had reached what is now South Africa. Genetic studies, including ancient DNA, show a large movement of people who mixed to varying degrees with the hunter-gatherer and herding peoples they met, whose own languages, such as the click languages of southern Africa, were often displaced.",
 [('Routes and timing','Researchers debate whether speakers moved mainly along rivers and the coast, or through a rainforest opened up by a drier climate around 2,500 years ago, and how much spread was migration versus local people adopting the language and way of life.'),
  ('Older ideas','Colonial-era writers sometimes used the expansion to argue that Bantu speakers were recent arrivals in southern Africa with no prior claim to land; this "empty land" idea has been rejected by historians and archaeologists, who show long settlement by both earlier and Bantu-speaking peoples.')],
 ['wiki:Bantu_expansion','wiki:Bantu_languages'],img='Bantu expansion',end=500),
X('benin-walls','The Walls of Benin: earthworks longer than the Great Wall?',H,'confirmed',2,1460,['archaeological','textual'],
 "The Iya are networks of ditches and earth banks around Benin City and across Edo State in southern Nigeria. The great inner moat and rampart around the city, about 12 km long, was built around 1460 under the Benin kingdom's obas; the much more extensive rural earthworks, which mostly marked village and field boundaries, may have begun as early as about 800 CE. The combined length of all the earthworks has been estimated at around 16,000 km, which led Guinness World Records to describe the linear earthworks of Benin and Isha as the longest of the pre-mechanical era. Much was damaged after the British punitive expedition of 1897 and by later urban growth; the Iya are on Nigeria's tentative list for World Heritage.",
 [('Bigger than the Great Wall','Popular articles often say the walls were four times longer than the Great Wall of China. Archaeologists stress that they are many separate earthworks of different ages, mostly modest boundary ditches, so the comparison is misleading even though the total effort was enormous.'),
  ('Overlooked achievement','Researchers such as Patrick Darling, who surveyed them from the 1960s, argued that the Iya were long ignored in world history because of colonial assumptions about African societies.')],
 ['wiki:Benin_Moat','wiki:Kingdom_of_Benin'],img='File:Benin City Walls.jpg',dateNote='Rural earthworks possibly from c. 800 CE; inner city moat c. 1460'),
X('tula-toltecs','Tula and the Toltecs: the real city behind the Aztecs\' golden age legend',H,'confirmed',2,950,['archaeological','textual (colonial chronicles)'],
 "Tula, in the modern Mexican state of Hidalgo, was the capital of the Toltecs, flourishing between the fall of Teotihuacan and the rise of the Aztecs, roughly 900 to 1150 CE. Its most famous monument is the Pyramid of Quetzalcoatl (Temple B), topped by four-metre basalt columns carved as warriors. The Aztecs later looked back on Tollan and the Toltecs as a golden age of wisdom and craftsmanship: 'Toltec' came to mean a skilled artisan, and Aztec rulers claimed Toltec descent. Aztec stories told of a priest-king, Topiltzin Quetzalcoatl, who was driven out of Tollan and departed eastwards, promising to return. Tula collapsed around 1150 and was later looted by the Aztecs for relics.",
 [('Tula and Chichen Itza','Tula\'s architecture and imagery closely resemble parts of Chichen Itza in Yucatán, more than 1,000 km away. Older theories said Toltecs conquered the Maya city; many archaeologists now see shared ideas and trade, and debate which way influence flowed.'),
  ('Myth and history','Some historians doubt how much of the Aztec picture of the Toltecs reflects the real Tula, suggesting "Tollan" was an idealised name used for several great cities, including Teotihuacan.'),
  ('The returning god','The story of Quetzalcoatl\'s promised return was later linked to Cortés\'s arrival. Many historians now think this link was largely created after the conquest (see the related entry).')],
 ['wiki:Tula_(Mesoamerican_site)','wiki:Toltec'],img='Tula (Mesoamerican site)',end=1150),
X('el-mirador','El Mirador: a giant Maya city that flourished centuries before the Classic age',H,'confirmed',2,-600,['archaeological','lidar survey'],
 "Deep in the rainforest of northern Guatemala lies El Mirador, a Maya city of the Middle and Late Preclassic period (about 1000 BCE to 250 CE). Its La Danta complex is one of the most massive pyramids in the world by volume, and raised white causeways (sacbeob) up to 40 metres wide link it with other cities across the Mirador-Calakmul basin. It reached its height between about 300 BCE and 150 CE and was largely abandoned before the famous Classic cities such as Tikal reached their peak. Since 2003 the archaeologist Richard D. Hansen has led large investigation and conservation projects there, and airborne lidar has mapped hundreds of related settlements under the forest.",
 [('Changed understanding','For much of the 20th century the Preclassic Maya were thought to be simple villagers. El Mirador and similar sites showed that monumental cities and states existed centuries earlier, pushing back the story of Maya civilisation.'),
  ('Why it collapsed','Proposed reasons for its decline include deforestation and soil loss (huge amounts of lime plaster required burning trees), drought, and political change. The relative weight of these factors is debated.')],
 ['wiki:El_Mirador'],img='El Mirador',end=150),
X('paquime','Paquimé (Casas Grandes): adobe towers, ballcourts and macaw farms in the desert',H,'confirmed',2,1200,['archaeological'],
 "Paquimé, also known as Casas Grandes, in the northern Mexican state of Chihuahua, was the largest and most complex settlement of its region between about 1200 and 1450 CE. Its builders, linked to the Mogollon culture, raised multi-storey adobe apartment blocks with some 2,000 rooms, and built a water system with channels, reservoirs and drains. It combined features of the Pueblo world of the American Southwest with Mesoamerican ones such as I-shaped ballcourts and platform mounds. Rows of adobe pens held scarlet macaws, imported from far to the south and bred for their sacred feathers, which were traded north. Paquimé was burned and abandoned around 1450; it became a UNESCO World Heritage Site in 1998.",
 [('Outpost or homegrown','Early excavator Charles Di Peso thought Paquimé was founded by Mesoamerican merchants (pochteca) as a trading outpost; later researchers mostly see it as a local society that adopted Mesoamerican ideas. The debate is a good example of shifting views on how cultures influence each other.'),
  ('Fall','Why it was burned and abandoned is unknown; conflict, drought and the breakdown of trade networks have all been proposed.')],
 ['wiki:Casas_Grandes'],img='Casas Grandes',end=1450),
X('manco-capac','Manco Cápac: the first Inca, son of the Sun',M,'myth-legend',2,1200,['textual (colonial chronicles)','oral tradition'],
 "Inca tradition, recorded by Spanish and mestizo chroniclers after the conquest, names Manco Cápac as the founder of the Inca dynasty and of Cusco, perhaps in the early 13th century. In one legend, told by Garcilaso de la Vega in 1609, the Sun god Inti sent his children Manco Cápac and Mama Ocllo up from the waters of Lake Titicaca with a golden staff, instructing them to settle where it sank into the ground, which it did at Cusco. In another, four brothers and four sisters, the Ayar siblings, emerged from a cave called Tambo Toco at Pacaritambo; during the journey the other brothers were turned to stone or sealed in a cave, and Ayar Manco went on to found Cusco. Inca rulers used these stories to present themselves as descendants of the Sun.",
 [('Real person?','Some historians think Manco Cápac may reflect a real leader of a small group that settled in the Cusco valley; others treat him as purely legendary. There are no contemporary records.'),
  ('Political myth','Scholars note that the two versions served different purposes: the Titicaca story linked the Incas to the ancient sacred lake and the older Tiwanaku world, while the cave story tied them to local ancestral places around Cusco.')],
 ['wiki:Manco_Cápac','wiki:Inca_mythology'],img='Manco Cápac',dateNote='Legendary; perhaps early 13th century'),
X('coyote-trickster','Coyote: the trickster who shaped the world and brought death',M,'myth-legend',2,1800,['oral tradition','ethnographic'],
 "Coyote is one of the best-known figures in the traditional stories of many Indigenous peoples of western North America, from the Great Basin and California to the Plateau and the Southwest. He is a trickster: clever and foolish, greedy and generous, a creator and a troublemaker. In various traditions Coyote steals fire for people, places the stars carelessly in the sky, releases animals or diseases, gives humans their customs, and in several stories decides that the dead should not come back to life. Stories were often told only in winter. Coyote shares many traits with Raven of the Pacific Northwest and with tricksters elsewhere, such as Anansi in West Africa and Maui in Polynesia.",
 [('Universal trickster','Comparative mythologists, following writers such as Paul Radin and Carl Jung, have seen the trickster as a worldwide human pattern that expresses the disorder and creativity of life. Critics warn that such comparisons can flatten very different cultures into a single type.'),
  ('Teaching stories','Many Indigenous storytellers emphasise that Coyote stories teach by bad example, showing the consequences of greed, pride and breaking rules.')],
 ['wiki:Coyote_(mythology)','wiki:Trickster'],img='Coyote (mythology)',dateNote='Oral traditions of great age; mostly recorded from the 19th century'),
X('cowherd-weaver-girl','The Cowherd and the Weaver Girl: lovers separated by the Milky Way',M,'myth-legend',2,-600,['textual','folk belief','astronomical'],
 "In Chinese tradition, Zhinü the weaver girl, a heavenly being identified with the star Vega, falls in love with Niulang the cowherd, a mortal identified with Altair. Because their love is forbidden, they are separated on either side of the Silver River, the Milky Way, and allowed to meet only once a year, on the seventh day of the seventh lunar month, when magpies form a bridge across the sky. The earliest known reference to the two stars as weaver and ox-herd is in a poem of the Classic of Poetry, more than 2,600 years old. The story is celebrated as the Qixi festival in China, Tanabata in Japan and Chilseok in Korea, when people traditionally made wishes, especially for skill in needlework and for a good marriage.",
 [('Stars to story','Scholars see the tale as growing from early star-watching: Vega and Altair do lie on opposite sides of the Milky Way, and the festival falls when they are high in the evening summer sky.'),
  ('Swan maidens','Folklorists note the similarity of some versions, in which the cowherd hides the robe of a bathing celestial maiden, to "swan maiden" tales found across Eurasia, suggesting very old shared story patterns.')],
 ['wiki:The_Cowherd_and_the_Weaver_Girl','wiki:Qixi_Festival','wiki:Tanabata'],img='The Cowherd and the Weaver Girl',dateNote='Earliest reference in the Classic of Poetry (c. 11th to 7th century BCE)'),
X('garuda','Garuda: the giant bird of Vishnu and enemy of serpents',M,'myth-legend',2,-500,['religious text','iconographic'],
 "Garuda is the king of birds in Hindu, Buddhist and Jain traditions, the mount (vahana) of the god Vishnu, shown as a giant bird or as a man with wings and a beak. In the Mahabharata he is born from an egg laid by Vinata; to free his mother from slavery to the serpents (nagas), the children of her rival sister, he steals the nectar of immortality (amrita) from the gods, and becomes the eternal enemy of snakes. Garuda spread with Indian religions across South-East Asia: he appears at Angkor and Prambanan, and today Garuda is part of the national emblems of India, Indonesia and Thailand, and the name of Indonesia's national airline.",
 [('Real birds','The image may draw on large birds of prey such as eagles and kites; the name is also associated with the greater adjutant stork in India.'),
  ('Thunderbirds and serpent-slayers','Comparative mythologists note similar giant birds that fight serpents in many cultures, such as the Thunderbird of North America and the eagle and serpent of Norse myth. Some see a shared Indo-European or even older pattern; others see independent responses to real predatory birds and snakes.')],
 ['wiki:Garuda','wiki:Nāga'],img='Garuda',dateNote='Mentioned in Vedic texts; main myth in the Mahabharata (compiled c. 400 BCE to 400 CE)'),
]
# --- part 4: Mesopotamia and Egypt; prehistoric and medieval Britain and Europe
E+=[
X('uruk','Uruk: the first great city, home of writing and of Gilgamesh',H,'confirmed',1,-3500,['archaeological','textual (cuneiform tablets)'],
 "Uruk, today the ruin mound of Warka in southern Iraq, was the leading city of Sumer in the 4th millennium BCE and gave its name to the Uruk period. By about 3100 BCE it may have had 50,000 residents, with tens of thousands more around it, making it probably the largest urban settlement in the world. Its Eanna temple district has produced the earliest known proto-cuneiform tablets, mostly administrative records of goods and labour, together with masterpieces such as the Warka Vase and the Mask of Warka. In Mesopotamian tradition Uruk was the city of the hero-king Gilgamesh, who the Sumerian King List says ruled there around the 27th century BCE, and of the goddess Inanna. The city was inhabited for some 5,000 years, until around the time of the Islamic conquest. The Bible calls it Erech.",
 [('Did Gilgamesh exist?','Many historians think Gilgamesh may have been a real early king of Uruk around 2700 BCE whose deeds were turned into legend, but no inscription of his own has been found.'),
  ('Why cities began','Scholars debate whether cities like Uruk grew from irrigation agriculture, temple economies, trade, or simply the gathering of people in a fertile delta. Uruk-style pottery and buildings found far up the Euphrates suggest an early network of colonies or trading posts, the "Uruk expansion".')],
 ['wiki:Uruk','wiki:Uruk_period','wiki:Warka_Vase'],img='Warka Vase',end=650),
X('merneptah-stele','The Merneptah Stele: the first mention of "Israel"',H,'confirmed',2,-1208,['inscription','archaeological'],
 "This granite stele, discovered by Flinders Petrie at Thebes in 1896 and now in the Egyptian Museum in Cairo, records the victories of the pharaoh Merneptah (reigned 1213-1203 BCE), mainly over the Libyans. Near the end, a short passage about a campaign in Canaan says: 'Israel is laid waste, his seed is not.' A sign in the hieroglyphs marks Israel as a people rather than a city or land. It is the earliest known textual mention of Israel, and the only one from ancient Egypt. Petrie himself considered it his most famous find.",
 [('What it tells us','Most scholars take it as evidence that a group called Israel lived in Canaan by about 1208 BCE. How this fits with the biblical accounts of the Exodus and conquest is debated: some see support for an early Israel, others note that the stele says nothing about Egypt or an exodus.'),
  ('Alternative readings','A minority of scholars have proposed other readings of the name, such as Jezreel or a different people, but these are not widely accepted. Some researchers also identify Israelites in nearby battle reliefs at Karnak; this is disputed.')],
 ['wiki:Merneptah_Stele','wiki:Merneptah'],img='Merneptah Stele'),
X('dendera-zodiac','The Dendera zodiac: an ancient sky map and the controversy over its age',H,'confirmed',2,-50,['archaeological','astronomical','inscription'],
 "The Dendera zodiac is a round sandstone relief from the ceiling of a chapel dedicated to Osiris on the roof of the temple of Hathor at Dendera, Egypt. It shows the night sky: Egyptian constellations together with the Babylonian-Greek signs of the zodiac, such as Taurus and Libra, held up by goddesses and falcon-headed figures, with the positions of planets and two eclipses. In 1821 the antiquities dealer Sébastien Louis Saulnier had it cut out with saws, jacks and gunpowder and shipped to Paris, where Louis XVIII bought it; it has been in the Louvre since 1922 and a cast replaces it at Dendera. The astronomer John H. Rogers called it 'the only complete map that we have of an ancient sky'. The Louvre dates it to about 50 BCE.",
 [('The "Dendera Affair"','In the early 19th century some French scholars argued from the star positions that the zodiac was many thousands of years old, a claim that seemed to challenge the biblical chronology. Champollion, after deciphering hieroglyphs, read names showing that the chapel was Greco-Roman, ending the debate for most scholars.'),
  ('Lost-knowledge claims','Some alternative writers still argue that the zodiac encodes much older astronomical knowledge, precession or past catastrophes. Egyptologists see it as a late synthesis of Egyptian and Mesopotamian-Greek astronomy.'),
  ('Return to Egypt?','In 2022 the Egyptologist Zahi Hawass launched a petition calling for the zodiac, the Rosetta Stone and other objects to be returned to Egypt.')],
 ['wiki:Dendera_zodiac','wiki:Dendera_Temple_complex'],img='Dendera zodiac'),
X('hallstatt','Hallstatt: the salt mine that named an age',H,'confirmed',2,-800,['archaeological'],
 "In a narrow Alpine valley above Lake Hallstatt in Austria, people have mined salt for more than 3,000 years, one of the oldest salt-mining sites in the world. Salt was precious for preserving food, and the wealth it brought is visible in the prehistoric cemetery discovered in 1846 by the mine official Johann Georg Ramsauer, who excavated over a thousand graves; around 1,300 burials are now known, rich in bronze, iron, amber and glass. The finds gave their name to the Hallstatt culture of central Europe (about 1200 to 450 BCE), linked with Proto-Celtic and early Celtic peoples. Because salt preserves organic material, the mines have also yielded tools, leather, textiles and even food remains of the miners.",
 [('The first Celts?','The Hallstatt culture is often called "Celtic", but scholars debate when and where Celtic languages arose; some argue they spread from the Atlantic west rather than from the Alpine region.'),
  ('A heritage landscape','The village and its region became a UNESCO World Heritage Site in 1997. Today Hallstatt faces overtourism, partly because it was copied in a replica town in China in 2012.')],
 ['wiki:Hallstatt','wiki:Hallstatt_culture'],img='File:Hallstatt - Salt mine, 1977 (02).jpg',dateNote='Salt mining from the Bronze Age; Hallstatt culture c. 1200-450 BCE'),
X('amesbury-archer','The Amesbury Archer: a man from the Alps buried near Stonehenge',H,'confirmed',2,-2300,['archaeological','isotopic','radiocarbon'],
 "In May 2002, during building work at Amesbury, about 5 km from Stonehenge, archaeologists found the grave of a man who died around 2300 BCE, aged about 35-45. He was buried with more objects than any other early Bronze Age grave in Britain: around 100 items, including flint arrowheads, stone wrist-guards for archery, copper knives, Beaker pots and the earliest gold objects yet found in England. Oxygen isotopes in his tooth enamel showed he grew up in central Europe, probably the western Alps. He had a damaged knee and a painful jaw abscess. A younger man buried nearby, possibly a relative, had the same unusual foot bones. His skeleton is in the Salisbury Museum.",
 [('King of Stonehenge?','Newspapers called him the "King of Stonehenge". Archaeologists caution that Stonehenge\'s main stones were probably already standing before he was born, though he may have come to visit or work there.'),
  ('Metalworker and migrant','Because he arrived as the first metal objects appeared in Britain, some suggest he was valued for knowledge of metalworking. Ancient DNA later showed that Beaker-associated people from the continent largely replaced Britain\'s Neolithic population within a few centuries, placing him in a much larger migration.')],
 ['wiki:Amesbury_Archer','wiki:Bell_Beaker_culture'],img='Amesbury Archer'),
X('seahenge','Seahenge: a Bronze Age timber circle revealed by the sea',H,'confirmed',2,-2049,['archaeological','dendrochronology'],
 "In 1998 shifting sands on the beach at Holme-next-the-Sea in Norfolk, England, exposed a ring of 55 oak posts surrounding an upside-down oak tree stump, its roots in the air. Tree-ring dating showed that the trees were felled in the spring or early summer of 2049 BCE. A second, nearby timber circle (Holme II) was built at the same time. Marks on the wood show that at least fifty different bronze axes were used. The purpose is unknown; one idea is that the upturned stump was used to expose a dead body to the elements. In 1999 English Heritage excavated the timbers to save them from the sea, and they are now displayed at King's Lynn. Holme II was left in place.",
 [('Excavation protests','The removal led to protests by local people who wanted it to stay as an attraction and by modern pagans and druids who saw it as a sacred site that should be left to the sea. Archaeologists argued it would otherwise be destroyed within years.'),
  ('A world upside down','The inverted tree has been interpreted as a symbol linking the underworld and the sky, a theme found in later myths of world trees. This is speculative.')],
 ['wiki:Seahenge'],img='Seahenge'),
X('lewis-chessmen','The Lewis chessmen: walrus-ivory warriors from a Scottish beach',H,'confirmed',2,1175,['archaeological','museum objects','art historical'],
 "In 1831 a hoard of 93 game pieces was found on the Isle of Lewis in the Outer Hebrides: 78 chessmen, 14 plain draughtsmen and a buckle, mostly carved from walrus ivory between about 1150 and 1200. The kings, queens, bishops, knights and warders (rooks) have expressive faces; the queens rest their cheeks on their hands, and some warders bite the tops of their shields like berserker warriors. They are commonly thought to have been made in Trondheim, Norway, when the Hebrides were under Norwegian rule, and to have been buried by a merchant. Today 82 pieces are in the British Museum and 11 in the National Museum of Scotland; a missing warder, unknowingly kept by an Edinburgh family since 1964, sold at auction for £735,000 in 2019.",
 [('Where were they made?','Besides Trondheim, scholars have suggested Iceland, where a bishop\'s ivory-carver is recorded, or the Hebrides themselves. The question is linked to arguments over national heritage.'),
  ('Discovery stories','Accounts of the find differ: a local legend says a farm worker found them in a sand dune and fled thinking they were elves or demons. Museum researchers now think the hoard was found at Mealista rather than at Uig Bay.')],
 ['wiki:Lewis_chessmen','wiki:Berserker'],img='Lewis chessmen',dateNote='Carved c. 1150-1200; found 1831'),
X('cerne-abbas-giant','The Cerne Abbas Giant: a chalk figure of uncertain age',H,'disputed',2,900,['archaeological','OSL dating','textual'],
 "The Cerne Abbas Giant is a 55-metre figure of a naked man with a club cut into a chalk hillside in Dorset, England. Its first written record dates only from the late 17th century, which led some historians to think it was cut then, perhaps as a parody of Oliver Cromwell. Others identified it with the Roman hero Hercules or a pagan god. In 2021 the National Trust announced results of optically stimulated luminescence dating of soil samples, indicating that the figure was first made between about 700 and 1110 CE, in the late Anglo-Saxon period, possibly close to the founding of nearby Cerne Abbey in the 10th century. Evidence shows parts of it have been altered and recut over time. Local folklore links it with fertility.",
 [('Saxon, Roman or 17th century?','The OSL results point to a late Anglo-Saxon origin, but researchers note that the figure might have been changed later and that its silence in medieval records is still puzzling. One suggestion is that it could have been a depiction of a Saxon god, perhaps one mentioned in local legends of the abbey; this is speculative.'),
  ('Folk belief','For centuries couples hoping for children are said to have visited the figure. Such customs are hard to date and may be much more recent than the giant itself.')],
 ['wiki:Cerne_Abbas_Giant','wiki:Hill_figure'],img='Cerne Abbas Giant',dateNote='OSL dates suggest c. 700-1110 CE; first recorded late 17th century'),
X('long-man-wilmington','The Long Man of Wilmington: an ancient-looking giant cut in the 16th or 17th century',H,'confirmed',2,1550,['archaeological','textual'],
 "The Long Man of Wilmington is a 72-metre outline of a human figure holding two staves on the steep slope of Windover Hill in East Sussex, England, designed to look in proportion when seen from below. It was long thought to date from the Iron Age or even the Neolithic. But an archaeological investigation in 2003 indicated that the figure was probably first cut in the early modern period, in the 16th or 17th century. Today its outline is marked with white-painted concrete blocks. Locally it was once called the Green Man. With the Cerne Abbas Giant, it is one of England's two major human hill figures.",
 [('Ancient or not','Its dating is a good example of how monuments that "look" prehistoric can be surprisingly recent; for many years writers linked it to ley lines, pagan gods or prehistoric astronomy.'),
  ('Fringe ideas','Alternative writers have seen it as a surveyor holding measuring rods, a gateway guardian or a figure aligned with sunrise; these ideas lack supporting evidence.')],
 ['wiki:Long_Man_of_Wilmington','wiki:Hill_figure'],img='Long Man of Wilmington',dateNote='Probably 16th-17th century (2003 investigation)'),
X('arthurs-grave-glastonbury','King Arthur\'s grave at Glastonbury (1191): a monastic discovery or publicity stunt?',H,'disputed',2,1191,['textual (medieval chronicles)','archaeological'],
 "In 1184 a great fire destroyed Glastonbury Abbey in Somerset. In 1191, while the monks were rebuilding, they announced that they had found the grave of King Arthur and Queen Guinevere in their cemetery. According to Gerald of Wales, writing a few years later, the abbot Henry de Sully had the ground searched and the diggers found, about 5 metres down, a hollowed oak trunk holding two skeletons, with a lead cross inscribed in Latin: 'Here lies buried the famous King Arthur in the Isle of Avalon'. The bones were later reburied in a black marble tomb in front of the high altar in 1278, in the presence of King Edward I. The tomb was lost when the abbey was dissolved in 1539. The cross was drawn by the antiquary William Camden but has since disappeared.",
 [('Publicity stunt (mainstream)','Most historians think the discovery was staged or at least embellished to attract pilgrims and money for rebuilding after the fire, and perhaps to please the Plantagenet kings by showing that Arthur was dead and would not return to lead the Welsh.'),
  ('A real early burial','Excavations in the 1960s by Ralegh Radford found evidence that a hole had been dug and an early grave removed at the spot the monks described, suggesting they did dig up an old burial. Whose it was cannot be known.'),
  ('Avalon','The claim that Glastonbury was the Isle of Avalon, and later legends that Joseph of Arimathea brought the Holy Grail there, made the town a centre of Arthurian and alternative spirituality that continues today.')],
 ['wiki:Glastonbury_Abbey','wiki:Avalon'],img='Glastonbury Abbey'),
]
